<BaseLayout title="Water Damage Mitigation Process | Bay Area Guide" description="A clear breakdown of professional water mitigation: inspection, moisture mapping, extraction, drying equipment, monitoring, and verification."><article class="article-page shell"><p class="eyebrow">Field note</p><h1>A practical water-mitigation breakdown</h1><p class="lede">This process overview preserves the useful topic of the former project-breakdown URL without presenting an undocumented customer project as evidence.</p><h2>1. Establish what happened</h2><p>Confirm the water source is stopped or identify the trade needed to stop it. Note when the loss began and where water is visible.</p><h2>2. Map the affected area</h2><p>Inspection follows likely migration paths into flooring, wall bases, ceilings, and adjacent rooms.</p><h2>3. Extract accessible water</h2><p>Standing water is removed before controlled evaporation and dehumidification begin.</p><h2>4. Set and monitor drying</h2><p>Air movement and dehumidification are adjusted as material and environmental conditions change.</p><h2>5. Verify and document</h2><p>The drying conclusion is supported by readings and documented conditions—not simply a room that looks better.</p></article></BaseLayout>
